escaped_string>\"}\n\nWe need to escape double quotes if any. In our code, we have single quotes, so no issue. But we have backslashes? No backslashes in code. But if we use newlines as \\n, we are already escaping newlines. Actually in the JSON string, we need to represent newlines as \\n literal characters, not actual newlines. So in the string we are writing, each \\n is two characters: backslash and n. When JSON parser reads it, it will interpret \\n as newline character. So we need to ensure that the string we provide contains \\n sequences. We are writing them as literal backslash-n in the string representation.\n\nThus in the final JSON, the implementation value will be a string with escaped newlines.\n\nWe should also ensure that the code uses proper indentation; we've used spaces.\n\nNow, we need to output the entire response as JSON. We'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    attempts = case.get('attempts', [])\\n    max_attempts = case.get('max_attempts', 0)\\n    n = len(attempts)\\n    if n == 0:\\n        decision = 'retry'\\n        next_attempt = 0\\n        memory_append = None\\n    else:\\n        last = attempts[-1]\\n        if last['success']:\\n            decision = 'stop'\\n            next_attempt = n\\n            memory_append = {'reflection': last['reflection'], 'signature': last['failure_signature']}\\n        else:\\n            if n >= max_attempts:\\n                decision = 'stop'\\n                next_attempt = n\\n                memory_append = {'reflection': last['reflection'], 'signature': last['failure_signature']}\\n            else:\\n                prev_signatures = [a['failure_signature'] for a in attempts[:-1]]\\n                if last['failure_signature'] in prev_signatures:\\n                    decision = 'stop'\\n                    next_attempt = n\\n                    memory_append = {'reflection': last['reflection'], 'signature': last['failure_signature']}\\n                else:\\n      